  "query_preview": "..\nThis discussion will cover the mid morning update and the rest \nof the afternoon. \n\nIso to perhaps sct showers/storms today, mainly south of Tucson\nand along the International Border. Main threats with today's\nactivity would be gusty winds in excess of 35 mph, brief heavy\nrain, and lightning. Any activity will quickly wane after sunset\nas we stabilize. \n\nMore activity is expected Wednesday as CAMs show more sct \ncoverage across the area. Coverage areas generally from Tucson \nMetro south and east. Main threats will continue to be gusty \nwinds (which could be stronger in excess of 40 mph) and heavy\nrain. With the storms a little more organized on Wed, there is\nmore of a threat of localized flash flooding. 12Z HREF gives a\n10% prob of 3\" totals in Santa Cruz County. \n\nLate in the week and into the weekend the tropics will begin to\ncome into play along with a deepening trough over the Pacific \nNorthwest. Tropical Storm Marie, currently centered well south \nof Baja, will be moving northwestward over the next few days. \nThe NHC track keeps it well west of Baja as it travels \nnorthward, but there is some uncertainty in the track after \nFriday. Some models favor a more northward track rather then \nnorthwestward. This will depend on how far south the \naforementioned upper level low travels. However even with the \nuncertainty in the track ensembles are in fairly good agreement \nwith an increase in moisture from the tropics beginning as early\nas Thursday. Expect monsoon thunderstorm activity to ramp back \nup starting Thursday into the weekend. By Friday mean PWAT \nvalues are 1.5\"-1.8\" from Tucson westward. PWAT values near 2\" \nin far western Pima County are not out of the question. This \nincrease in moisture will lead to a risk for heavy rainfall and \nflash flooding. WPC has southeast Arizona under a slight risk \nfor excessive rainfall Thursday and Friday. \n\nHigh temperatures will remain a few degrees below normal through\nthe rest of the work week then warm a few degrees to around\nnormal this weekend.",
